p.src:
Choose the normalized in–out convention $\ket{\mathrm{in}}=\frac{\ket{u}+i\ket{d}}{\sqrt2},
\qquad
\ket{\mathrm{out}}=\frac{\ket{u}-i\ket{d}}{\sqrt2},$ with $\sigma_y\ket{\mathrm{in}}=+\ket{\mathrm{in}},
\qquad
\sigma_y\ket{\mathrm{out}}=-\ket{\mathrm{out}}.$ Interchanging the labels or reversing the $y$-axis changes a sign convention, not the underlying construction. With Equation [eq:l3-in-out-states], the unique Hermitian matrix with these eigenvectors and eigenvalues is $\boxed{
\sigma_y=
\begin{pmatrix}
0&-i\\
i&0
\end{pmatrix}_{\{u,d\}}.}$ It is easy to verify rather than rederive: $\begin{aligned}\begin{pmatrix}0&-i\\i&0\end{pmatrix}
\frac1{\sqrt2}\begin{pmatrix}1\\i\end{pmatrix}
&=\frac1{\sqrt2}\begin{pmatrix}1\\i\end{pmatrix},\\
\begin{pmatrix}0&-i\\i&0\end{pmatrix}
\frac1{\sqrt2}\begin{pmatrix}1\\-i\end{pmatrix}
&=-\frac1{\sqrt2}\begin{pmatrix}1\\-i\end{pmatrix}.\end{aligned}$ The factors of $1/\sqrt2$ normalize the eigenvectors but do not affect their eigendirections.

